\begin{proof}
Let $X=\prod_\beta X_\beta$ be Hausdorff and fix $\alpha$. Choose $a_\beta\in X_\beta$ for every $\beta\neq\alpha$, which is possible because each factor is nonempty. For each $x\in X_\alpha$, let $s(x)\in X$ be defined by
\[
s(x)_\alpha=x,\qquad s(x)_\beta=a_\beta\quad(\beta\neq\alpha).
\]
The image $s(X_\alpha)$ is a subspace of $X$, hence is Hausdorff. The map $s:X_\alpha\to s(X_\alpha)$ is continuous because each coordinate map is continuous, and its inverse is $\pi_\alpha|_{s(X_\alpha)}$, which is continuous. Thus $X_\alpha$ is homeomorphic to a Hausdorff subspace of $X$, and is therefore Hausdorff.\end{proof}
